<body>
    <h1>Object Detection</h1>
    <div class=3D"Section1">
        <p>Object Detection is designed as a modular, extensible platform f=
or video and image analysis. At its heart is the VisionAnalyzer class, whic=
h orchestrates the process of loading media, detecting objects, and perform=
ing high-level vision tasks. This class is designed to work with various ob=
ject detection and vision models, allowing for flexibility and future expan=
sion.</p>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p>Optimized Media Processing:<br>
To improve efficiency, the system is designed to process media in a single =
pass where possible. For videos, this means seeking through the file once, =
performing both object detection and vision tasks on each relevant frame. T=
his approach significantly reduces I/O operations and processing time.</p><=
/li>
<li>
<p>Parallel Processing:<br>
The architecture incorporates parallel processing capabilities to take adva=
ntage of multi-core systems. Frame extraction and analysis can be distribut=
ed across multiple threads or processes, dramatically improving performance=
 for large videos. This is particularly useful for time-consuming tasks lik=
e object detection and complex vision analysis.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Efficient Frame Handling:<br>
To optimize performance, the system uses frame buffers as parameters throug=
hout the processing pipeline. This approach avoids unnecessary disk I/O and=
 repeated image decoding, as frames are kept in memory and passed between d=
ifferent stages of analysis. This is particularly beneficial when multiple =
analysis steps are performed on the same frame.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Metadata Management:<br>
The system generates and manages metadata throughout the processing pipelin=
e. This includes frame information, object detection results, and vision an=
alysis outputs. The metadata is structured to allow for efficient querying =
and analysis, and is saved in a JSON format for easy integration with other=
 systems.</p></li>
</ol>
<p></p>
<p>The Object Detection class is designed with flexibility and configurabil=
ity at its core. Upon initialization, it accepts various parameters that de=
fine its behavior, including the types of models to use for object detectio=
n, vision analysis, and language processing. These parameters can be passed=
 directly or retrieved from a global configuration. This design allows for =
easy customization of the detection process without modifying the class its=
elf. The class also sets up essential attributes such as frame processing p=
arameters and progress tracking mechanisms, ensuring that it's ready to han=
dle a wide range of detection scenarios right from instantiation.</p>
<p>One of the key strengths of the Object Detection class is its ability to=
 process both images and videos seamlessly. The class incorporates logic to=
 determine the type of input media and routes it to the appropriate process=
ing pipeline. For images, it performs a straightforward processing routine.=
 For videos, it implements a more complex frame extraction and selection pr=
ocess. This dual capability makes the class versatile enough to be used in =
various applications, from analyzing single photographs to processing lengt=
hy video sequences, all within the same consistent interface.</p>
<p></p>
<h1 id=3D"ObjectDetection-LegacyFrameProcessing">Legacy Frame Processing</h=
1>
<h4 id=3D"ObjectDetection-ModelIntegration">Model Integration</h4>
<p>The class is architected to integrate seamlessly with additional vision =
and language models, enabling more comprehensive analysis of visual data. T=
his multi-modal design facilitates tasks such as scene understanding or fin=
e-grained object classification, complementing the primary object detection=
 capabilities. Vision models are employed to expand the range of detection =
and classification tasks beyond basic object recognition, while language mo=
dels can be used to generate descriptions of detected scenes or answer ques=
tions related to visual content.</p>
<p>However, the current integration primarily focuses on processing individ=
ual frames, meaning each detection task operates independently without cons=
idering the relationships between frames. While this approach allows for fl=
exibility in swapping detection models (e.g., YOLOv8, GroundingDINO, Robofl=
ow), it does not inherently support temporal continuity or object tracking =
across time. The system=E2=80=99s reliance on frame-by-frame processing lim=
its its ability to maintain object identities or track movements in dynamic=
 video content, which is essential for applications requiring object detect=
ion over time. Thus, the current model integration is suitable for single-f=
rame analysis but lacks the temporal cohesion needed for more advanced, dyn=
amic video analysis.</p>
<p>In summary, while the architecture allows for a wide range of model inte=
grations, the focus on frame-based processing limits its ability to leverag=
e the temporal context inherent in video data. Future integrations should c=
onsider spatiotemporal models (e.g., Florence 2 and Pixtral) to address the=
se limitations.</p>
<h4 id=3D"ObjectDetection-ObjectDetectionExecution">Object Detection Execut=
ion</h4>
<p>At the core of the class is its object detection execution capability, w=
hich is responsible for managing the detection process across multiple fram=
es. This component leverages the specified object detection model and a Tas=
kManager to handle the execution of detection tasks across frames or images=
. The current design allows for flexible integration of different detection=
 models, but it is built on a frame-centric processing paradigm, treating e=
ach frame as an independent entity.</p>
<p>While the TaskManager efficiently distributes tasks and can parallelize =
the execution for better resource utilization, the lack of temporal awarene=
ss in the detection process is a key limitation. The system processes each =
frame independently, and there is no built-in mechanism for tracking object=
s across consecutive frames or maintaining consistency in object identity. =
This limits the class=E2=80=99s utility in dynamic video environments where=
 objects may move in and out of frames or change appearance over time.</p>
<p>The object detection execution is model-agnostic, which allows for easy =
swapping or upgrading of models. However, in dynamic video scenarios, this =
component would benefit from the integration of tracking algorithms or spat=
iotemporal models to capture object behavior over time. This would enable m=
ore accurate detection in scenes where objects move, interact, or change st=
ate across frames.</p>
<h4 id=3D"ObjectDetection-FrameProcessing">Frame Processing</h4>
<p>The frame processing component of the class is responsible for selecting=
 frames for object detection in video inputs. The current implementation us=
es a combination of frame sampling and content-based selection techniques t=
o balance computational efficiency with thoroughness. Specifically, frames =
can be selected based on fixed intervals, or the system can analyze frame d=
ifferences to focus on frames where significant visual changes occur. This =
approach ensures that the system can reduce redundant processing in static =
video segments, concentrating computational resources on the most relevant =
frames.</p>
<p>However, this frame processing technique is optimized for individual fra=
me selection and does not account for temporal relationships between frames=
. The reliance on frame differences and sampling means the system is not aw=
are of how objects persist, move, or change across consecutive frames. This=
 makes the class less effective in scenarios where object continuity over t=
ime is essential, such as in dynamic scenes, action recognition, or video s=
ummarization tasks.</p>
<p>Moreover, the absence of object tracking across frames further limits it=
s use in more complex video analysis tasks, such as tracking an object=E2=
=80=99s trajectory or understanding interactions between objects over time.=
 While the current design is efficient for applications like security foota=
ge review or monitoring static scenes, it lacks the temporal depth required=
 for analyzing fast-moving objects or dynamic video content. Integrating ob=
ject tracking algorithms or spatiotemporal models (such as those provided b=
y Florence 2 and Pixtral) would significantly enhance the system's ability =
to handle such use cases.</p>
<h3 id=3D"ObjectDetection-TemporalTracking">Temporal Tracking</h3>
<p>To address these limitations, we propose integrating spatiotemporal mode=
ls (such as Florence 2 and Pixtral) and object tracking algorithms to allow=
 for continuous object detection and tracking across time. This will ensure=
 that the system can maintain object identities across frames and improve a=
ccuracy in dynamic video environments.</p>
<h4 id=3D"ObjectDetection-Overview">Overview</h4>
<p>In our current object detection framework, we perform object detection o=
n individual frames in a video. This method relies on frame sampling or cal=
culating differences between consecutive frames to optimize processing. How=
ever, this approach lacks the ability to maintain object identity and track=
 objects over time. The primary limitation is that each frame is processed =
independently, without consideration of temporal continuity, which is criti=
cal for accurately detecting and tracking objects in dynamic video scenes.<=
/p>
<hr>
<h4 id=3D"ObjectDetection-LimitationsoftheCurrentSystem">Limitations of the=
 Current System</h4>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Frame-by-Frame Processing</strong>:<br>
Our current system processes individual frames independently, either by sam=
pling frames at a specific rate or based on frame difference calculations. =
While this reduces redundancy in frame selection, it causes each frame to b=
e treated as a standalone image, with no relationship to previous or subseq=
uent frames. This frame-based method works well for static scenes but fails=
 to capture object behavior over time.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>No Object Tracking</strong>:<br>
The system does not track objects across frames. Even though we detect obje=
cts in individual frames, there is no mechanism to maintain object identity=
 across frames. This means that the same object detected in one frame may n=
ot be recognized as the same object in a subsequent frame, leading to redun=
dant or inconsistent results, especially in dynamic or fast-moving scenes.<=
/p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Loss of Temporal Context</strong>:<br>
By processing each frame independently, the system loses important temporal=
 context. In dynamic video scenarios, the relationship between objects in o=
ne frame and their position or state in subsequent frames is critical for u=
nderstanding object behavior, motion, and interaction with other objects. T=
his is particularly important in use cases like action recognition, sports =
analysis, and surveillance, where we need to track how objects change over =
time.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Suboptimal for Dynamic Video Content</strong>:<br>
In videos with significant object motion or interaction (e.g., autonomous d=
riving, sports, or security footage), the current system=E2=80=99s frame-by=
-frame approach struggles to maintain consistent detection. As objects move=
 across frames, their appearance may change, or they may become partially o=
ccluded, causing inconsistent detection results. Without temporal tracking,=
 the system cannot accurately capture these objects=E2=80=99 trajectories o=
r behaviors.</p></li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h4 id=3D"ObjectDetection-Objectives">Objectives</h4>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Object Detection Over Time</strong>:<br>
We aim to implement object detection over time, enabling the system to proc=
ess sequences of frames and account for the movement and persistence of obj=
ects across a video. By analyzing multiple frames as a cohesive sequence, w=
e can ensure that objects are detected and tracked throughout the video, ma=
intaining temporal consistency.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Object Tracking Across Frames</strong>:<br>
We will integrate an object tracking algorithm, ensuring that objects detec=
ted in one frame are associated with the same objects in subsequent frames.=
 This will allow us to track the movement and state of objects over time, p=
reserving their identity throughout the video. The result will be consisten=
t object tracking even in dynamic or fast-changing environments.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Spatiotemporal Processing Using Florence 2 and Pixtral</strong>:=
<br>
Rather than processing individual frames, we will explore spatiotemporal mo=
dels like Florence 2 and Pixtral. These models are capable of understanding=
 both spatial and temporal dimensions, allowing for object detection over a=
n entire sequence of frames. By integrating these models, we can leverage t=
heir ability to capture object behavior and movement across time, leading t=
o more accurate and consistent results.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Improved Accuracy in Dynamic Scenes</strong>:<br>
By maintaining object continuity across frames, we will improve detection a=
ccuracy in dynamic scenes, such as those involving fast-moving objects or m=
ultiple interacting entities. This will be critical in use cases like surve=
illance, sports analysis, and autonomous vehicle navigation, where objects =
are in motion and must be tracked consistently.</p></li>
</ol>
<hr>
<h4 id=3D"ObjectDetection-DesignApproach">Design Approach</h4>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Spatiotemporal Model Integration (Florence 2 and Pixtral)</stron=
g>:<br>
We will integrate Florence 2 and Pixtral, which are designed to process vid=
eo sequences by understanding both the spatial and temporal aspects of the =
content. These models can process sequences of frames simultaneously, captu=
ring how objects evolve and move over time. By leveraging their ability to =
analyze entire video segments, we can ensure that object detection is both =
accurate and temporally consistent.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p><strong>Florence 2</strong>: This vision-language model is capable of mu=
ltimodal processing and can handle video sequences for object detection and=
 tracking. Florence 2 will be adapted to process multiple frames together, =
ensuring that objects are consistently detected and tracked across time.</p=
></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Pixtral</strong>: Pixtral specializes in high-performance video =
analysis, making it ideal for detecting and tracking objects over time. By =
integrating Pixtral=E2=80=99s capabilities, we will be able to maintain obj=
ect identity and track objects across frames in a dynamic video environment=
.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Object Tracking Integration</strong>:<br>
In addition to spatiotemporal models, we will integrate an object tracking =
algorithm that allows us to maintain object identities across frames. This =
will involve associating detected objects in each frame with their previous=
 detections, ensuring that the system tracks their movement and behavior ov=
er time.</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>We will explore tracking methods like <strong>DeepSORT</strong>, which b=
uilds on detected bounding boxes from the spatiotemporal models and tracks =
object identities across frames. This will enable robust tracking in scenar=
ios with multiple moving objects, even when objects become occluded or chan=
ge appearance.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Handling Temporal Data</strong>:<br>
Instead of treating frames as isolated images, we will process them as sequ=
ences, leveraging models like Florence 2 and Pixtral that are designed to o=
perate on time-ordered data. By feeding multiple frames to the model at onc=
e, we can capture both the spatial features within each frame and the tempo=
ral dependencies between them. This will allow the model to understand how =
objects move, change, and interact over time.</p></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Post-Processing and Evaluation</strong>:<br>
After detection and tracking, we will implement post-processing to evaluate=
 the results over time. This includes refining the detection bounding boxes=
 across frames to ensure consistency, handling occlusion cases where object=
s are temporarily hidden, and smoothing object trajectories to eliminate su=
dden jumps or false detections. Metrics such as tracking accuracy, object i=
dentity preservation, and temporal consistency will be used to evaluate and=
 optimize the system.</p></li>
</ol>
<ol start=3D"1">
<li>
<p><strong>Integrate Spatiotemporal Models</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Load and configure Florence 2 and Pixtral to process sequences of video =
frames.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Ensure that the models are capable of handling both spatial and temporal=
 dimensions, extracting features that account for object movement over time=
.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Implement mechanisms to batch video frames for processing, feeding them =
to the models as sequences rather than isolated images.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Implement Object Tracking</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Integrate an object tracking algorithm (e.g., DeepSORT) into the framewo=
rk, allowing the system to maintain object identities across frames.</p></l=
i>
<li>
<p>Ensure that detected objects are associated with previous detections, an=
d implement tracking logic to handle objects moving in and out of the frame=
 or becoming occluded.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Handle Dynamic Video Scenarios</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Optimize the system to handle videos with dynamic scenes, where objects =
may move quickly, interact with each other, or become occluded.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Test and refine the system=E2=80=99s ability to track objects in these s=
cenarios, ensuring that the system can handle both static and dynamic scene=
s effectively.</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p><strong>Evaluation and Testing</strong>:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Define evaluation metrics such as tracking consistency, object identity =
preservation, and detection accuracy across time.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Test the system using a variety of video datasets, including those with =
dynamic and fast-moving objects.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Refine the system based on testing results, focusing on improving tempor=
al tracking accuracy and minimizing false positives or identity switches.</=
p></li>
</ul></li>
</ol>
<h2 id=3D"ObjectDetection-ModularExtensions">Modular Extensions</h2>
<p>To facilitate a modular Object Detection implementation, we=E2=80=99ll d=
ecompose the work into the following components:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Object Model: We will support a wide variety of object models out of the=
 box. For basic COCO types, we can do zero-shot image recognition, with exi=
sting implementations with the following models:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>YOLOv8</p></li>
<li>
<p>Grounding Dino</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p>Pre-Trained Object Model: Currently, we support fine-tuned models hosted=
 by Roboflow, accessible over HTTP. Future iterations may support other fin=
e-tuned models hosted locally or remotely.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Image Pre-Processing: These are a series of steps that can be applied to=
 the image processing function, such as zoom, crop, image combination, etc.=
</p></li>
</ul>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: py; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Apply p=
re-processing steps
        if pre_processing_steps:
            for step_name in pre_processing_steps:
                if step_name in self.pre_processing_steps:
                    cv_image =3D self.pre_processing_steps[step_name](cv_im=
age)</pre>
</div>
</div>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Vision Model: The vision model implementation. Currently we support the =
following models:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>Qwen-VL</p></li>
<li>
<p>Phi3</p></li>
<li>
<p>GLM-4V</p></li>
<li>
<p>CogVLM</p></li>
<li>
<p>Florence2 (coming soon!)</p></li>
</ul></li>
<li>
<p>Vision Post-Processing: These are a series of steps that can be applied =
after the vision model returns. This may include such tasks as LLM processi=
ng and custom code for LLM output. It is invoked in a similar manner</p></l=
i>
</ul>
<div class=3D"code panel pdl" style=3D"border-width: 1px;">
<div class=3D"codeContent panelContent pdl">
<pre class=3D"syntaxhighlighter-pre" data-syntaxhighlighter-params=3D"brush=
: py; gutter: false; theme: Confluence" data-theme=3D"Confluence"># Apply p=
ost-processing steps
result =3D {"response": response}
if post_processing_steps:
   for step_name in post_processing_steps:
      if step_name in self.post_processing_steps:
         result =3D self.post_processing_steps[step_name](result)  </pre>
</div>
</div>
<h1 id=3D"ObjectDetection-Configuration">Configuration</h1>
<p><code>object_model_type</code>: Specifies the object detection model to =
use. Possible values:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>"yolov8": YOLOv8 model, a state-of-the-art, fast, and accurate object de=
tection model.</p></li>
<li>
<p>"groundingdino": GroundingDINO model, which performs object detection wi=
th natural language queries.</p></li>
<li>
<p>"roboflow": Roboflow model, which allows integration with custom-trained=
 models on the Roboflow platform.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Default: "groundingdino"</p>
<p><code>vision_model_type</code>: Specifies the vision model for additiona=
l image analysis. Possible values:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>"qwen_vl": Qwen-VL model, a vision-language model for various visual und=
erstanding tasks.</p></li>
<li>
<p>"phi3": Phi-3 model, designed for visual reasoning and analysis tasks.</=
p></li>
<li>
<p>"glm4v": GLM-4V model, a vision-language model for understanding and des=
cribing images.</p></li>
<li>
<p>"cogvlm": CogVLM model, a cognitive vision-language model for advanced v=
isual understanding.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Default: "glm4v"</p>
<p><code>llm_model_type</code>: Specifies the language model for text-based=
 tasks. The format is "provider::model". Currently supported provider:</p>
<ul>
<li>
<p>"ollama": Ollama-based language models. The model name follows the "::" =
separator.</p></li>
<li>
<p>Examples: "ollama::llama2", "ollama::mistral", etc.</p></li>
</ul>
<p>Default: "llama3"</p>
<p><code>frame_difference</code>: Threshold for determining significant cha=
nges between video frames.<br>
Default: 50<br>
Used when <code>use_frame_difference</code> is True. Frames with a differen=
ce percentage above this threshold are processed.</p>
<p><code>use_frame_difference</code>: Flag to enable/disable frame differen=
ce-based sampling.<br>
Default: False<br>
When True, the system uses content-based sampling instead of regular interv=
al sampling.</p>
<p><code>frame_sampling_per_minute</code>: Controls the rate of frame sampl=
ing in videos.<br>
Default: 30<br>
Determines how many frames per minute of video are processed when not using=
 frame difference.</p>
<p><code>text_threshold</code>: Confidence threshold for text detection.<br=
>
Default: 0.5<br>
Used by some models to filter text detections based on confidence.</p>
<p><code>box_threshold</code>: Confidence threshold for bounding box detect=
ions.<br>
Default: 0.5<br>
Used to filter object detections based on the model's confidence score.</p>
<p><code>roboflow_confidence</code>: Confidence threshold for Roboflow mode=
ls.<br>
Default: 50<br>
Similar to <code>box_threshold</code>, but specific to Roboflow models.</p>
<p><code>roboflow_overlap</code>: Overlap threshold for Roboflow models.<br=
>
Default: 25<br>
Used to determine how much overlap is allowed between detected objects.</p>
<p><code>disable_preproc_auto_orient</code>: Flag to disable automatic imag=
e orientation correction.<br>
Default: False<br>
When False, the system attempts to correct the orientation of images based =
on EXIF data.</p>
<p><code>match_config</code>: A mapping of object class IDs to human-readab=
le labels.<br>
This dictionary maps numeric class IDs to string labels (e.g., "0": "person=
", "1": "bicycle", etc.).<br>
Used to convert model outputs into human-readable object names.</p>
<p><code>llm_model_request_timout</code>: Timeout for language model reques=
ts in seconds.<br>
Default: 120<br>
Sets a limit on how long the system will wait for a response from the langu=
age model.</p>
<p>These configuration parameters provide fine-grained control over the obj=
ect detection process, allowing users to adjust the system's behavior witho=
ut modifying the code. The combination of flexible frame processing options=
 and model-specific parameters makes this system adaptable to a wide range =
of object detection scenarios.</p>
<p></p>
    </div>
</body>
</html>
